Dreesen Jos wrote:

> My Microvax ( actually a VAXstation/GPX minus the graphics
> boards) does not boot beyond "2.."


Well it should eventually give up and
issue an error message. Either your
drive is not bootable (in which case
you have no OS or it is corrupt or you
booted it incorrectly), or you drive
cannot be seen (the RQDX3 is faulty,
the RQDX3 is not on the Qbus properly,
the cabling from the RQDX to the drive is
not properly seated, the drive is
incorrectly jumpered, the drive is dead).

In BA23 chassis (the small one) I always
found that pushing both the connectors
home on the drive end, followed by pushing
carefully home on the bulkhead end helped
a great deal. I don't think I ever
had a dead drive or board - it was
*always* a cabling SNAFU.

 
> So : is the RD53 shot ? Any possiblilty to check the drive ?


A uVAX III CPU helps here. You have SHOW QBUS
and SHOW DEVICE which will get you a long way
towards debugging problems. With a uVAX II
CPU, it's harder.

> When the GPX-2 is in the machine, booting stops at "A". 
> Could this be due to a missing keyboard / mouse ?


Plenty of uVAX II and VS II manuals can
be found at:

http://208.190.133.201/decimages/moremanuals.htm

Test A is the video test. I don't know
whether it passes or fails when the
keyboard and mouse are not present.
I'd be surprised if it hangs forever,
although broken hardware can make almost
anything happen.

If the GPX boards are in there, the console
will use them - so that's where the
output will go. Are you seeing it hang
on the monitor or are you looking at the
output using a serial console?


Antonio



