Eric Smith wrote:
> 
> Bob Willard <BobwBSGS@attbi.com> writes:
> > I think you are hosed.  IIRC, the CIPCA (both -AA and -BA versions)
> > can only share a CI with other nodes that support 4K packets; that's
> > why HSC50s and older CI host adapters are disallowed.  {ISTR that some
> > HSC70s and/or some CIXCDs/CIBCAs had to be brought up to rev for 4K
> > packets, but I won't swear to that.}  The CIPCA was never tried with
> > a CI20, and I'll give good odds that CI20s don't use 4K packets.
> 
> That still sounds like a software problem.  I doubt that the CIPCA
> hardware or firmware is incapable of dealing with smaller packets; it's
> probably just a matter of the VMS driver wanting to use 4K packets.
> 
> Can the CI780 handle 4K packets?  If so, since the CI20 design is similar,
> it probably can as well.
> 
> The CI20 doesn't normally use 4K packets.  To determine whether it is
> capable of dealing with them, I'd have to study the CI20 print set and
> the CI20 microcode sources.
> 
> But given that there doesn't appear to be any way that I can get the
> source to the CIPCA driver, maybe I'll have to build my own PCI CI
> adapter using an FPGA.  Building RP06 and TU77 emulators is a higher
> priority, though.

IIRC, the 4K packet limit was built into the CIPCA microarchitecture;
I don't recall if it was mere on-board firmware, or buried more deeply
in programmable sequencers.  Note that while the CIPCA uCode can be
reloaded from the host, this does lead to the restriction when booting
through the CIPCA that both old and new uCode work well enough to boot.

I don't think the CI780 could support 4K packets; that's why the CIPCA
could not cohabitate with any CI780/CI750/CIBCI or HSC50 nodes.  And,
I'll bet that the CI20 had the same restriction.
-- 
Cheers, Bob


