Eric Smith <eric-no-spam-for-me@brouhaha.com> writes:
> The "smarts" of the CI20 are on the IPA, which plugs into the KL10
> Ebus and Cbus, modules M3001, M3002, and M3003.  These are
> definitely not the same as any modules used on the CI780, since the
> latter is an SBI device.  SBI is not even similar to the KL10 Ebus
> and Cbus.

Paul Repacholi <prep@prep.synonet.com> writes:
> No, the 'smarts' of the CI780/750/20 are in the box on the other end
> of the cables. Enough AMD29xx to make Tom Edison smile :) The SBI/CMI
> or E/Cbus connection is made with different card sets.

I don't have any CI780 or CI750, or prints thereof, so they may well be
as you describe.  But I do have a CI20 and its print set (MP01903), so I
can be quite certain that the CI20's "box on the other end" has no
substantial smarts.

The CI20 consists of the IPA (M3001, M3002, and M3003) modules plugged
into the KL10 I/O backplane (in place of an RH20), and the separate box
containing the L0100 and L0109 modules.

The M3001 contains a microcoded controller built using AMD29xx bit slice
components.  The L0100 and L0109 have nothing of the sort; they are just
the low-level link hardware.  The smarts of the CI20 are definitely in
the IPA, which is KL10-specific.

The NIA20 uses the same IPA (M3001-M3003) running different microcode,
with a single L0072 module in the separate box.  The L0072 is the
low-level link hardware for Ethernet.


