Rich,

The dimensions you've given correspond to those of a standard later-model
baby-AT tower power supply.  Because of the differeneces in power on-off signal
conventions between AT and ATX, don't even consider ATX.

In addition to the standard pair of AT power connectors, your power supply also
has a 3.3v connector which looks like the other two.  Intel pushed for this
modification to AT power supplies on the supposition that the whole world would
move to 3.3v PCI cards, rather than the 5v which has prevailed to this day.

One question about the power supply: Does it have a long usually black cord
leading to an on-off switch on the front of the computer, or does it have its
own built-in on-off switch, or does it have a 2- or 3-wire lead to a header on
the motherboard?  If the former two, some sort of standard baby AT power supply
would do.  If the latter, then you need a sort of hybrid AT power supply which
came into use during the transition to ATX.  This "hybrid AT power supply" was
used in IBM and several other brands of late Pentium-class computers.

Also, if a "hybrid AT", the wiring harness from the main logic board to the
front panel will have two leads to a momentary push button switch, same as the
ATX on-off switch. 

Then the front panel fan: Check its voltage rating.  It is probably 12v, like
most.  If you can easily get another 12v fan to test, try it.  If the fan has
failed, the system won't even give a whimper when you press the on-off switch.

... Ben 

