>>>>> On 12 Apr 2002 15:16:48 -0700, Greg Finn ("Greg") writes:
 Greg> Since many language specific workstation companies failed, I find a
 Greg> management-specific failure argument unconvincing.  Especially when there
 Greg> were at least three companies selling lisp machines that all failed.

I'll give you a hint: what market was targeted (to the extent that any
of those companies had a marketing stragegy at all), and what happened
to that market?  I'm most familiar with Symbolics, so here's another hint: 
Can you count how many executive officers Symbolics went through?
What was that all about and how did it affect the company's success?
Another hint: can you say something about the company's financial problems
and eventual bankruptcy in relation to its investments in real-estate?
Finally, regarding the Xerox Lisp Machines (which I would not say were
as general purpose as the Symbolics machines, since for example they
could not run all the different languages at once): please tell us about
how Xerox has been so successful in marketing its conventional workstations
and other computer products, and about how the relevent technical
innovations were the cause of that whopping success story.

 >>> Nothing necessarily prevents one from building a Lisp/Pascal/Ada ...
 >>> microprocessor.  But for it to be commercially successful, it would have to
 >>> (1) compete cycle for cycle with other general-purpose micros and (2)
 >>> support OS/app portability.

Well, that's clearly false, since special-purpose hardware and software
is commercially successful.  If you modify your claim to say that a
*general-purpose* workstation has to compete cycle for cycle and
have a compatible OS, that would at least make some sense.
But I would temper the "commercially successful" part, because 
there might be something so compelling about the software that
customers would put up with different (maybe even slower) hardware.
Eventually, relative performance would be too much to put up with, 
but the factors in the speed competition are described by curves.

 Greg> I believe you are concentrating exclusively on the hardware.
 Greg> I was not.  

Then what's all this talk about "microprocessors" and speed?

 Greg> My lisp machine was from Xerox and it certainly could not
 Greg> compete as a Fortran box or a C box.  It was language-specific
 Greg> when booted.  It did not support app portability, let alone OS
 Greg> portability.

The Symbolics machine ran, for example, the X Window System server,
which is written in C.  Later versions of the workstation were
headless and so that was the only display that you got.

 Greg> Maybe the hardware in a Symbolics machine could run Fortran and C just as
 Greg> fast as a 68010, or whatever was the best 68000 system at that time. 
 Greg> Did it?

Well, initially when the Lisp Machine came out (when manufacturing was
only at MIT) the best microprocessor was the 6502, or the Z80 if you
prefer.  When the first Symbolics machines came out, the best 68000
system was the 68000 (no memory management).
But I thought you weren't focusing on the hardware?

You're the one making the claim that FORTRAN/C performance was the factual
basis of the problem, so it's incumbant on you to back up your claim.
Please provide benchmarks showing that there was a performance problem.
Then show how that was what was important to the customers, and how that
failure led to the commercial demise.

A number of thesis have been written about the failure of Symbolics,
by the way.  Also, the people involved in the company are available
to interview.  (Although you're apparently not interested in believing them...)

 Greg> I do know Symbolics machines were initially six feet tall, quite expensive
 Greg> by comparison to a normal workstation and needed a good deal of air
 Greg> conditioning.  Perhaps that had something to do with their demise also?

Well, initially there were no other workstations - the Lisp Machine
was one of the very first workstations (late 1970s), and it was also
the first commercially available system.  (Yes, before Xerox.) 
The word "workstation" had not yet been coined so nobody called them that.
By the mid-1980s they came in a variety of sizes, but most models
were either mini-fridge or desk-side sized, same as everything else.
Later models were a chip, which could be plugged into a VME machine
(desk-size or pizza box, or your SUN box) or into a Macintosh.
The last version of the Lisp Machine runs on the DEC Alpha.

By the way, "Expensive by comparison" is a management issue,
not a technology issue.

However, even that is debatable in some sense, since customers 
reported that one Lisp Machine seat replaced several seats of
programmers on conventional machines.   (Bear in mind that we're
talking about "general purpose" applications, a few of which I
mentioned in an earlier message.)

What the Lisp Machine was definitely not cost-effective for was solving
trivial problems.  The customers were those with "impossible" problems,
typically having failed multiple times using conventional computers.
The problems, however, were "general purpose" computing.


