Performance maybe?  The DS10, for instance, had remarkably unimpressive IO
with IDE disks through its built in IDE interface.  The 500au may have the
same poor performance.  There's a test package here:

ftp://saf.bio.caltech.edu/pub/software/benchmarks/mybenchmark.zip

  which has some simple code for testing read/write speed on VMS disks (and also
other disks on Unix systems).  There are some tables in there with
results from various systems for comparison purposes.

I suspect that the reason you had to bump up the priority on your other
application
when the ftp server was moving data was that the IDE read/write method was both
slow
and was generating CPU interrupts relatively frequently at a higher priority
than your
user level application.  If that's true then if you set up a RAMDISK on your VMS
system you'll probably find that the ftp server load is no longer significant.
For what it's worth, on our late lamented VMS DS10 with U2W SCSI
the FTP server never seemed to load the system significantly even when very
large
files were being downloaded.

