John E. Malmberg wrote:

> Kevin McQuiggin wrote:
> 
> First search http://www.google.com/ to search the comp.os.vms group for 
> KZQSA.  That will tell you how to configure it properly for the q-bus.


I have the manual here, so this may save some seacrhing.
W13...W9 set A21...A17 for
the Qbus memory. The requirement is that
it not overlap with any other cards
Qbus memory region (many have none).
The default is 01000000...01377777
(W10 installed, others out)

Leave W8 alone (i.e. uninstalled).

Since you have an old console, you need to
run SYSGEN from OpenVMS (or bit-hack by hand).

You need to feed it all the cards you have
then it tells you what you need to set.
For example, given a KDA50, a DELQA and
the KZQSA you would do:

$ MC SYSGEN
sysgen>CONFIG
device>UDA
device>QNA
device>QZA
device>^Z
(that's control-Z)
and it spits out the list:
Device: UDA     Name: PUA        CSR: 772150     Vector: 154
Device: QNA     Name: XQA        CSR: 774440     Vector: 120
Device: QZA     Name: PKA        CSR: 761300*    Vector: 300*

(I stripped the last column ... it plays
havoc with my news reader!)

I recommend you run it twice, once without the
KZQSA (QZA above) and once with. Notice that,
if you are unlucky, some of the *other* cards
may have to be changed to accomodate the
KZQSA!

The CSR jumpers are W7...W1
For the above setting of 761300
you would need 0001011 (i.e. W4,W2 & W1
in, the rest out).

Finally W14,W15,W16 set the
KZQSA SCSI ID (it must be different
to that of any device you hang off here).
W16 is the LSB ... the default setting
is 7 (ie all jumpers installed).

> The tape drive may do the cycling simply because there was a power on 
> reset internally, and does not indicate a correct connection.


Every tape I've come across does exactly that.

> Then you will probably need VAX/VMS 5.4 to recognize the KZQSA.  I seem 
> to recall there was a patch kit needed for VAX/VMS 5.3 to use it.


The manual claims V5.3-2 ...

> I would recommend using a more modern version of OpenVMS, such as 7.3.


... I'd definitely recommend that too. Although
on a MicroVAX II with RD drives I might stick
with V6.2.

> 
> You will not be able to use the KZQSA as a boot device with CPUs older 
> than a VAX 4000 Model 300.


Internally to DEC, there was a little "boot stub" 

that could be put on an RD drive (or even a floppy),
that would go and boot of a KZQSA. If you can
find that, then maybe you can boot old
processors from a KZQSA ...

 

Antonio

