I just saw so many names of old friends and co-workers that I had add my thoughts,
if only for
the historical record.  I helped install both the first and last DECSYSTEM-20's in
the New York metropolitan area.
My involvement with DEC as student, employee. or customer starts with the PDP-8
and continued to the bitter end.

Digital failed to realize that improvement in personal computers would cause them
to compete with "minicomputers" that would seal its doom.

This blindness was caused by arrogance: its own success (mostly), a failure to
look outside of itself for ideas, and, I've
come to conclude, too much clout was given to people whose computing experience
was formed in the 1960's and had
too little of a forward-looking vision (i.e. too much trust in people over 40).

Companies that fail to learn why Digital failed in the end are doomed to repeat
that experience.

Pat Sweeney





