Susan,

Not a stupid question at all.  According to Intel's recommendation, the CPUs are
special only due to the fact that they need to have matched steppings to run
with the greatest reliability.  You cannot tell what the processor stepping is
until you run it with utility software, but the production code is marked on the
top of the processor case.  All processors with the same production code have
the same stepping.  The production code is usually 5 alphanumeric characters,
for example SL236.

The industry got away from special processors a long time a go.  DEC and other
name-brand manufacturers ordered extremely large lots of processors of the same
production code, used some in systems, kept others for upgrades.

One other thing.  Dual and multi processor systems often need voltage regulator
modules.  Which model of system do you have?  I'll check the documentation I
have.  Let me know which production code is stamped on the processor.  I still
may have a matching one... Ben Myers

