Performance analysis of Lisp systems is very complicated, mostly
because there are lots of things that can be measured (on purpose 
and accidently).  Benchmarking Lisp systems is the subject of a number
of graduate thesis, including the well-known PhD thesis by Gabriel.

While looking around for some numbers for you, I ran across some
benchmarks done in 1991 by David Gadbois and Akira Kurihara.
The program is for ISQRT on very large numbers (thousands of
digits of precision).  Several different algorithms were tested,
including the built-in ISQRT operator and two different faster
versions (mostly testing Lisp function calling; recursion, in 
one of the algorithsm) and Lisp's infinite precision arithmetic
(especially the FLOOR and ASH functions).

So I have some Lisp benchmarks (for square roots of numbers with 1000 
to 10,000 digits) in front of me showing the Symbolics machine being
at least twice as fast as the SPARCStation 1+.


