cstacy@theworld.com (Christopher C. Stacy) writes:

> >>>>> On 10 Apr 2002 23:10:26 -0700, Eric Smith ("Eric") writes:

[snip]

>  Eric> It was intended to support a lot of things.  Lisp was one but didn't
>  Eric> get particularly more attention from the designers than Fortran and Algol.
> 
> So say you, but Alan Kotok who designed the PDP-6 and PDP-10 
> with Gordon Bell says differently:
> 
>    "Indeed, that's me (without the R.). I was chief architect of the
>    PDP-10, many, many years ago. The architecture dated back to 1963
>    and the PDP-6, which I worked on with Gordon Bell. And, yes,
>    facilitating a good LISP implementation was an important
>    consideration."

He said "an", not "the".  My understanding is that there were differing
outside research groups that had influence.  Timesharing as opposed to batch
was another example.  But this is becoming a religious argument.

Machines designed to optimize execution of one particular high-level
language at the detriment of others have, as a rule, failed commercially.
The jack of all trades has won virtually every hand.

Yes, you can design a Lisp machine or Pascal machine, then write a Fortran
compiler in Lisp or Pascal.  For that matter, you can write one in TECO.
However, the difficulty of writing an OS and supporting suite of
applications is so great that portability across differing machine
architectures is vital.

Nothing necessarily prevents one from building a Lisp/Pascal/Ada ...
microprocessor.  But for it to be commercially successful, it would have to
(1) compete cycle for cycle with other general-purpose micros and (2)
support OS/app portability.

This is all old hat anyway.  Most machines do little but web surf.  Today
one should be building HTML micros.  One could then use Internet Exploder
for kernel debugging.


