It is different. You are refering to High Voltage Differential, and your 
remarks are correct for that kind of connections. However there are High 
Voltage Differential SCSI disks around too... You can not mix these kind 
of disks with other types of SCSI disks.

LVD was invented for performance reasons. It is available in 80 MB/sec, 
160 MB/sec, and even 320 MB/sec. Normal (UW) SCSI can only reach 40 
MB/sec. Connecting a LVD disk with a UW device on the same electrical 
SCSI bus will set the whole bus to UW.

There are SCSI controllers with two SCSI busses, one UW bus and one LVD 
bus. Both busses are connected by means of a SCSI bridge. This will have 
the effect of creating one logical SCSI bus with a maximum of 16 SCSI 
addresses (incl. the controller). The SCSI adresses on both busses 
should be unique For example you can not use SCSI address 2 on the UW 
bus as well as one the LVD bus. It can only be used on one of them.

