Bill Todd wrote:

> 
> Roger Ivie posted a response in IIRC comp.sys.dec (and perhaps not included
> here) stating that rotational speed tolerance was indeed the problem - had
> to be within 3% for format (else would over-run if it was spinning too
> fast), whereas the drive as shipped was only speced to within 10%.
> 

The early 8" drives were always a problem for formatting, irrespective
of vendor, because they were driven by an ac induction shaded pole motor
straight off the line. They were susceptable to line frequency
variation, as well as load. The lead screw assembly / stepper motor
mechanics were pretty crude as well. Most 8" floppies came formatted for
this reason. The later 1/2 height drives, with pancake brushless dc
motors, were much better and had much better spindle speed tolerance,
but by then, they were effectively obsolete.

It wasn't until dec rebadged the standard pc 1.2 meg drive (RX33 ?), for
Vaxmate initially, that format became a reliable procedure, though I
believe RX50 (correct me if i'm wrong) drives could format floppies
using XXDP, but that wasn't generally available to customers.

Chris


