> from performing well when executing the other languages?
> Then explain to me how that is what caused the company to fail.

Since many language specific workstation companies failed, I find a
management-specific failure argument unconvincing.  Especially when there
were at least three companies selling lisp machines that all failed.

I believe you are concentrating exclusively on the hardware.  I was not.  My
lisp machine was from Xerox and it certainly could not compete as a Fortran
box or a C box.  It was language-specific when booted.  It did not
support app portability, let alone OS portability.

Maybe the hardware in a Symbolics machine could run Fortran and C just as
fast as a 68010, or whatever was the best 68000 system at that time.  Did
it?

I do know Symbolics machines were initially six feet tall, quite expensive
by comparison to a normal workstation and needed a good deal of air
conditioning.  Perhaps that had something to do with their demise also?


