>> peripherals were made (and for which even fewer can still be found), and,
>> as mentioned, couldn't format its own floppies.
> 
> I just can't remember the rationale for this.  There were certainly
> piracy-protection efforts back then that involved special floppy formating,
> and that might have been part of the reason.

The rationale was that the format crammed so much into a track that the
drive had to be rotating very close to the specified rate (3%, IIRC). The
RX50 drive installed in computers was only specified for 10% speed
tolerance. It was possible to have an RX50 drive operating in spec which
could not format floppies because it was spinning too fast.
