On 9 Apr 2002, Christopher C. Stacy wrote:
> The main feature of the Lisp Machine systems was always the software,
> not the hardware, but there was management confusion about that, too.

People generally have a much greater commitment to software platforms than
to hardware platforms.  There is nothing wrong with seeking a customized
hardware platform to run a particular software platform, and very often
that is the right thing to do.

In the case of a PDP-10, it appears that x86 commodity hardware can run
PDP-10 software with a 30x hit over native x86 code, which is excellent
given that 100x is more typical for an emulator.  Put another way, for the
price of 1.5GHz x86 commodity hardware, you can have a 50MHz PDP-10.

Any hardware platform that can beat this on price/performance and still
run PDP-10 software is a superior platform for running PDP-10 software.
This could be a custom PDP-10 on a chip.  Or it could just be a different
commodity architecture that is more suitable for PDP-10 microcode.

The only thing that matters is what delivers the best price/performance
for the software.  Commodity hardware seems to cost about $15 per PDP-10
MIPS and the last time I priced custom PDP-10 systems they were more like
several thousand dollars per PDP-10 MIPS.

However, it may be possible to build a PDP-10 in custom hardware for less
than $15 per PDP-10 MIPS.

