I wrote
> It depends on your perspective.  But a VAX or Sun was certainly more
> general-purpose than a LISP machine.  Not enough people wanted LISP
> machines, so the engineering costs couldn't be amortized over enough
> units to make them both cost-effective and profitable.

cstacy@theworld.com (Christopher C. Stacy) writes:
> I'm not sure where you get the idea that the Lisp Machine companies
> folded due to engineering costs.  That's not what happened at all.
> The problem had more to do with mis-management of the companies
> in areas like real estate investment, and certain failures of focus.
> The main feature of the Lisp Machine systems was always the software, 
> not the hardware, but there was management confusion about that, too.
> It's quite a long and complicated saga, but it had very little to do
> with the technology itself.

That may all be true, but it was also the case that the market was very
limited because the lisp machine hardware was never competitive in terms
of price/performance ratio with other available choices, even if you DID
want to run LISP.  There were some applications for which this was not
an issue.

> In the context of a workstation, the Lisp Machine was certainly a
> fully "general purpose" system -- what would type of computing do 
> you imagine was not possible to do with it?

If you really want to maintain that the Lisp machines did not use
special-purpose processors, then please explain why they didn't simply
use commercially available (i.e., general-purpose) processors.  Hint:
it's because they wanted some specialized features that general-purpose
processors don't have.  In other words, they built special-purpose
processors.  Trying to argue that a Lisp machine is a general-purpose
computer because it can run Fortran is like trying to argue that an F16
is a a general-purpose aircraft because it can carry a commuter from
San Francisco to Los Angeles.

Sure, it's Turing-complete.  No question about it.  It's designed to run
Lisp efficiently, at the cost of putting it behind the curve for
everything else.  If you didn't want to run Lisp, it wasn't
cost-effective.  If you did want to run Lisp, it may have been
marginally cost effective early in the product life, but not for very
long thereafter.

I suspect that the Lisp machine companies would almost certainly have
been more successful if they'd just developed software for use on
conventional machines instead, and stayed out of the hardware business.

If the world had standardized on Lisp instead of C, perhaps LMI and
Symbolics would be where Intel or AMD are now, and Lisp processors would
be "general-purpose".  But it didn't happen.

The point I was trying to make was that building special-purpose
processors (even if we disagree on the exact definition of "special-
purpose") is almost always a losing proposition, because so much more
money is spent pushing the state-of-the-art of general-purpose
processors.  Usually by the time a special-purpose processor is designed,
debugged, and manufactured, general-purpose processors have advanced
enough in the interim that the special-purpose processor has very little
edge even for the task for which it has been optimized.

Special-purpose processors are occasionally the right
thing for certain niches.


