>>>>> On Tue, 09 Apr 2002 21:52:05 -0600, Ben Franchuk ("Ben") writes:

 Ben> Eric Smith wrote:
 >> I suspect that the Lisp machine companies would almost certainly have
 >> been more successful if they'd just developed software for use on
 >> conventional machines instead, and stayed out of the hardware business.
 >> 
 >> If the world had standardized on Lisp instead of C, perhaps LMI and
 >> Symbolics would be where Intel or AMD are now, and Lisp processors would
 >> be "general-purpose".  But it didn't happen.

 Ben>  But Lisp like Forth are so unlike most other languages that you have a
 Ben> large overhead getting a machine to map to the virtual machine code both
 Ben> languages use. Read somewhere a group of people ( 1980's???) made a lisp
 Ben> cpu on a chip. You could process lists really fast, but you could not
 Ben> add with it!

If you're thinking of the same "Lisp Machines" I'm thinking of, they had
high-performance floating point (as well as infinite precision arithmetic), 
and also were the premiere workstations for 3D graphics and animation.

There was more than one Lisp-on-a-chip project in the 1980s, though,
and some of them were just lab experiments concerned with some particular
aspect of Lisp, and so may have lacked good support for arithmetic.
I am thinking of the SCHEME-79 chip, for example (although I don't
remember anything about it and am too lazy to go look it up.)

But probably you are just repeating some nonsense that someone gave
you who didn't know what they are talking about.  It is an oft-repeated
myth that Lisp is not good at arithmetic.  This was debunked in the 1970s
when it was shown (for one thing) that the PDP-10 Lisp compiler
outperformed the PDP-10 FORTRAN compiler for arithmetic.


