cstacy@theworld.com (Christopher C. Stacy) writes:
> Finally, what is the basis for your implied premise that
> Lisp is not a general purpose language?

I reread my posts and don't believe that I ever said anything that
implies that.

But if you build a processor optimized to run Lisp, it's a special-
purpose processor.  And if you build a computer around that, it's
a special-purpose computer.  You seem to be the only person that
disagrees.

I'm not picking on Lisp.  If you build a processor or computer
optimized to run Forth, or Snobol, or Intercal, that would also be
a special-purpose processor or computer.  I'd even say the same
about a processor optimized to run C, except that C has such minimal
requirements that it's not clear that there are any reasonable
optimizations for that.  Most everything you can do to improve a
processor's performance running C code will apply equally well to
other languages.


