On Wed, 10 Apr 2002, Ben Franchuk wrote:
> Since LISTs uses lots of memory
> LISP was never practical on a machine with less than 256K words of
> memory thus untill after the 386 could people use LISP for non trival
> problems.

I respecfully suggest that you do not know what you are talking about.

Interlisp, which by any reasonable definition was the Godzilla among the
PDP-10 Lisp systems, ran in 160K words of memory.

Stanford Lisp 1.6, which for years was the standard, ran in a mere 13K.
MacLisp, which began to displace Lisp 1.6 in the mid-70s, ran in 47K,
although with a modest amount of effort it could be reduced to run in less
than 32K.

A vast amount of AI work was done in the 1970s and early 1980s on all
three.

There is not a single programming technique in Lisp that is not also
extensively used in non-trivial C programs, including lists.

My suspicion is that you're the victim of a bad book on Lisp, and as a
result haven't a clue as to how people used Lisp.

For the record, the first IMAP client was written in Lisp.  The complete
Lisp source code for an advanced, GUI based IMAP+SMTP client was about
200K bytes.  A reimplementation for the NeXT in C, taking advantage of all
the NeXT GUI facilities, was several times larger.

