bill@triangle.cs.uofs.edu (Bill Gunshannon) writes:

> Like compiling Ada on the VAX circa 1982. One compile made the
> machine unusable for other users for a couple of hours.  Probably
> one of the reasons why Ada left a bad taste in my mouth.  No other
> compiler I ever used on any other system had that drastic an effect
> on overall system performance.

That was an odd one... The problem was the compiler did all its
initialization at startup, and the init routines where with the code
that used the whatever.  Result was, the entire compiler was paged
through multiple time for a *tiny* bit of code to be executed. And
some did the same at the beginning of any block in the source :(

A few PSECTs got it all in on place in the image, and a HUGE speed up
for almost no change. It was boosted by ther change in VMS so you
'paged against the system' not against your own process. 

