 Eric> Tim Shoppa <shoppa@trailing-edge.com> writes:
 >> But to most embedded-type folks a LISP machine is not a special-purpose
 >> machine, it's a general-purpose machine.

 Eric> It depends on your perspective.  But a VAX or Sun was certainly more
 Eric> general-purpose than a LISP machine.  Not enough people wanted LISP
 Eric> machines, so the engineering costs couldn't be amortized over enough
 Eric> units to make them both cost-effective and profitable.

I'm not sure where you get the idea that the Lisp Machine companies
folded due to engineering costs.  That's not what happened at all.
The problem had more to do with mis-management of the companies
in areas like real estate investment, and certain failures of focus.
The main feature of the Lisp Machine systems was always the software, 
not the hardware, but there was management confusion about that, too.
It's quite a long and complicated saga, but it had very little to do
with the technology itself.

The Lisp Machines were strictly single-user workstations, so it doesn't
make total sense to compare them to the VAX, which used by most customers
as a timesharing system.  On the other hand, very atypically, we had a
farm of VAX-11/750s used as individual workstations at MIT, and they
were far less powerful than the Lisp Machines.  I don't remember if
the early SUN systems were typically single-user workstations (which
is what I saw) or timesharing machines.  But the Lisp Machine was also
a much more powerful machine than the SUNs.

In the context of a workstation, the Lisp Machine was certainly a
fully "general purpose" system -- what would type of computing do 
you imagine was not possible to do with it?

If it's the word "Lisp", maybe you're unaware that the "Lisp" Machine
also included FORTRAN, C, ADA, PASCAL, PROLOG, MACSYMA, and some other
languages, and that it interoperated over the network with all known
operating systems (that is, transparent network file access using the
foreign host's own native file system syntax and semantics, automatically
selecting the various protocols such as FTP, NFILE, NFS, etc; also
supporting all standard network protocols such as TELNET and RPC).

I am pretty sure the Lisp Machine was the first system to have the
attributes cited in the original message here (the original Xerox
machines were much less powerful by comparison), and it was the 
first such workstation to be available commercially.

When I say "powerful", I'm talking about number of features,
computing speed, memory size, disk size, network, graphics, 
and of course the software (which was the main thing).

Lisp Machines were used for an infinite variety of problems.

During the Gulf War, I remember watching on TV and being amused to
note that the bombing sorties (in particular, but along with lots of
other strategic operations) were being scheduled by a Lisp machine,
and the military was communicating over networks that were designed
using a Lisp Machine, and that even the TV network news graphics that
I was looking at were done on a Lisp Machine.  (Of course, Lisp Machines
were used for lots more than wars and television...that was just one
day when they happened to be simltaneously used for multiple purposes
in everybody's living room, so "general purpose" brought it to mind.)

What do you mean by "general purpose" that was lacking?
They weren't multi-user timesharing, and they weren't cheap, 
if that's what you mean.


