Jeff Campbell <jcampbell@ins-msi.com> writes about the inability of most
DEC floppy disk subsystems to format disks:
> Not piracy, but interchange. Not allowing customer formatting almost
> eliminates service calls for interchange problems.

Can't see why.  Floppy drives use open-loop positioning, usually with a
stepper motor.  So a drive that couldn't format properly due to
positioner or head alignment error will still have problems reading and
writing even with preformatted floppies.

In fact, the problems with writing can be worse, because you can wind up
with media that has the address fields and data fields at differing
radii, which is much harder to recover data from than a disk that has
been formatted and written on the same out-of-alignment drive.

I very much doubt that either piracy or interchange was the motivation.
I think the initial RX01 design would have been more complicated and
expensive if it had been designed to support formatting, so it was
simply a cost-cutting measure.  Then someone noticed that it also
allowed them to charge more money for formatted diskettes, and that led
them to deliberately leave out formatting capability in almost all
floppy subsystems they designed thereafter.  But of course this is only
supposition on my part.


