cstacy@theworld.com (Christopher C. Stacy) writes:

> >>>>> On 08 Apr 2002 18:56:43 -0700, Eric Smith ("Eric") writes:

>  Eric> I wrote:
>  >> Trying to use special-purpose hardware to compete with general purpose
>  >> CPUs is like trying to run in front of a steamroller.  You might be able
>  >> to do it for a short while, but you're going to get tired before the
>  >> steamroller does.  This is why LISP machines were ultimately
>  >> unsuccessful.

>  Eric> Tim Shoppa <shoppa@trailing-edge.com> writes:
>  >> But to most embedded-type folks a LISP machine is not a special-purpose
>  >> machine, it's a general-purpose machine.

>  Eric> It depends on your perspective.  But a VAX or Sun was certainly more
>  Eric> general-purpose than a LISP machine.  Not enough people wanted LISP
>  Eric> machines, so the engineering costs couldn't be amortized over enough
>  Eric> units to make them both cost-effective and profitable.

> I'm not sure where you get the idea that the Lisp Machine companies
> folded due to engineering costs.  That's not what happened at all.
> The problem had more to do with mis-management of the companies
> in areas like real estate investment, and certain failures of focus.
> The main feature of the Lisp Machine systems was always the software, 
> not the hardware, but there was management confusion about that, too.
> It's quite a long and complicated saga, but it had very little to do
> with the technology itself.

[snip]

None of the language-specific workstations was what one would call a
commercial success a far as I am aware.  Pascal, Lisp and Mesa machines
tried their hands at becoming successful and folded.  There were several
Lisp workstations.  For example, Symbolics, LMI and Xerox come to mind.
Something was obviously wrong with all of them commercially.

Many of these early workstations took a page out of the Xerox PARC method
and tried to optimize CPU performance for a particular language by
microcoding the architecture to suit that language.  While a Xerox Dandelion
chassis could run multiple languages, it did so by rebooting into another
machine architecture.

That was an attractive design option only so long as microprocessors
remained small and slow by comparison with a multi-chip, customized CPU
design.  Also, with an entirely new hardware/software architecture, it took
years to build a respectable operating system and suite of useful packages.
But by that time micros had passed through another generation or two of
advancement.  As 1980 rolled around it was already clear that 32-bit micros
such as the 68000 (@ 8MHz) would soon render bit-slice designs obsolete.

It's hard to beat the physics advantages of a single-chip microprocessor.
Eventually, even Cray couldn't beat that.


