> In article <DTiotGxQ0bj6-pn2-lWybcVJEsL2J@localhost>,
> Dave Weatherall <djweath@attglobal.net> wrote:
> >Where does one draw the line between 'timesharing' and 'multi-user'?
> >The -M in RSX11-M, after all. On the other hand, I always used to
> >think of the VAX as RSX in hardware. ie. lots of the stuff that was
> >done in s/w in RSX now done in h/w on VAX. Context switch support
> >comes to mind.
>
> And yet an '11 could handle twice the users of an "equivalent" VAX.

Much as I loved the 11 and felt that it provided a superior platform to VAX
for many tasks well into the '90s, I can't let such a sweeping statement
pass unquestioned.

First, it's a hardware-to-hardware comparison, which really requires that
other variables (principally, the software) be held constant.  Perhaps you
could compare RSX with VAXeln and get closer than what I assume you're doing
(comparing against VMS), but even then you're comparing a mature,
finely-honed OS with a far younger one.

And comparing any 11 system to VMS is comparing apples to oranges.  Virtual
memory allows the system to make use of all its resources to provide good
responsiveness under light load while avoiding the need to deal with
something like TKB to make the system run efficiently under heavy, diverse
loads with complex applications - but in the process you pay the price that
neither is in all ways as efficient as a well-designed overlay environment
where what gets loaded (and when) is under the control of the developer
rather than a generic paging manager.

So how things compare depends on what you're doing.  Perhaps you really
could run twice as many users on the 11 in many cases, but I suspect not if
they were linking applications, since TKB had to work considerably harder at
this than the VMS linker does.  And even if you could run twice as many
applications on Alpha today if you scrapped virtual memory and returned to
something like TKB, I suspect few would take you up on it.

- bill





