"Charlie Root" <root@acer.reistad.priv.no> wrote in message
news:_rqr8.51$Vv5.2594@nreader1.kpnqwest.net...
> At my college the DEC2065 tended to get slow at round 150 users, where
> around 100 would be active at any one time. It only got terminally
> bad at two occations; both under severe stress from a lot of different
> work.
>
I was one of the systems programmers for a well-known corporation which used
a 2040 in their accounting office.  Far from hundreds of users, we were
lucky to sustain more than 8 COBOL developers simultaneously (plus myself,
though I usually stuck to assembly language).  On the other hand, overnight
batch would outrun the 370/138 sitting next to the 2040.  For that matter,
the 2040 wuld still outdo the 370 during the day as well.  Those programmers
willing to switch enjoyed the TOPS vs. VM environment, though all came to
nought as the company made a non-technical decision to be an IBM only shop.
A year later the 2040 was sold to someplace in California.  And I eventually
wound up at another company on a 11/34, followed by an 11/780.  Out of all
those systems, the one I most liked to work on was...my own IMSAI with a
Z-80 running CP/M (a well-known RT-11 near clone).  It supported a maximum
of one user (sometimes none when smoke came out the back), as far as i'm
concerned the ideal developer environment.

I have to agree with Fred about 10/20 nostalgia.  They were good machines in
their day but time and selective memory have served to gloss over the
shortcomings.  I can't imagine why anyone would prefer a 10 or 20 over an
Alpha, or even a VAX (though anyone who ever used a MVax I could make a good
case for a 2020).
    Jack Peacock





