>> in the front end hardware (the Berkeley Bussiplexer) the 11/70 managed
>over
>> 70 users, the 11/780 croaked between 30 and 35.

>Wow.  That kind of surprises me, but it does sound like a fair comparison -
>assuming the VAX software was using the same memory management techniques
>(e.g., overlays) rather than virtual memory.  Certainly a hell of a lot more
>impact than the difference between 16 and 32 bit pointer-size overhead can
>account for.

The -11 was using split I&D, but I don't believe any of the commonly used
programs were large enough to require overlays and I'm not sure that 2BSD
supported them at this point (2.11 did). With 70 users in 2M the system was
swapping fairly heavily, but the most commonly used programs were running
with shared text and so their code tended to stay in core.

The VAX was using demand paged VM, but most of the programs were the same
as on the -11, so they had fairly small working sets and again the text
segments (code) tended to stay in real memory. There were a few large programs
in use (Ingres, for example) but people tended to avoid running them under
this kind of load. :->

I do think that this was still a fair comparison, since the whole point to
using a VAX rather than an 11 *was* the demand paging support.

ISTR that the 11 hardware could technically do demand paging, but with only
8 pages per segment, it would have been kind of pointless. PDP-11 UNIX *did*
use this capability to trap page faults near the stack (which grew down from
high memory in the UNIX memory model) and automatically grow the stack as
needed. The bourne shell also used this to allocate memory to the heap: it
would just use the memory and if it got a trap *then* it would call brk() to
grab some more memory.

Needless to say this caused problems on the first micro ports, since the 68000
and other micros of the day couldn't recover cleanly from memory faults... I
don't know what they did about the Bourne shell, but they used stack probes
in the subroutine preamble to make sure the stack was all there before trying
to use it.

Here's a longer discussion of the whole clever scheme:

http://yarchive.net/comp/bourne_shell.html

