Peter da Silva wrote:
> 
> 
> ISTR that the 11 hardware could technically do demand paging, but with only
> 8 pages per segment, it would have been kind of pointless. PDP-11 UNIX *did*
> use this capability to trap page faults near the stack (which grew down from
> high memory in the UNIX memory model) and automatically grow the stack as
> needed. The bourne shell also used this to allocate memory to the heap: it
> would just use the memory and if it got a trap *then* it would call brk() to
> grab some more memory.

Even some of the early high end models (11/40-45) had separate
instruction and data space of 64k bytes max each, with 2 sets of address
translation registers, 1 per space. Not quite harvard architecture as it
used the same physical address and data busses, but neat none the less.
This was mapped with a virtual address translation scheme that allowed
the 8 active memory pages per space to be of any size from 64 bytes to
8k bytes each. While such numbers seem miniscule now, you have to
remember that nearly all programs were written in macro and you can do a
lot with 64 k bytes in assembler + considering the coding efficiency of
the instruction set. If you really got stuck, you could always use
overlays to build any program size you like, which could be considered a
precursor technique to swapping.

One technique I found especially interesting (in 11M+) was the 'shuffler
run', a task which ran periodically to defragment the memory by moving
all active tasks down to recover all the holes, leaving all the free
space at the top.

Chris


