Mark Crispin <mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> writes:
> On the other hand, there is Lingling, which I believe to be the fastest
> TOPS-20 system ever at 15x KL speed (about the equivalent of a 45 MHz
> Intel) and put together for a cost of about $750.  I imagine that with a
> more suitable hardware base (the microcode incurs a 33x performance hit,
> which is actually pretty good -- 100x is considered more typical of an
> emulator), we could have a real kick-ass system.

While it is certainly true that a state-of-the-art (or nearly so) ASIC
implementation could be much faster than a simulator running on an x86
(or even an Alpha, Power4, etc.), it's not clear that the same
proposition would be true for an FPGA-based design.  The latter would
clearly be more cycle efficient, but it would be difficult to get its
cycle time better than 20 ns, vs. under 0.42 ns for a current P4 or 0.58
ns for a current Athlon.  This means that the FPGA would have to be on
average 34x more cycle-efficient than the simulator on an Athlon in
order to have better performance.  With enough work it could be done,
but the payoff is too small to justify it.

And by the time the FPGA solution was designed and tested, AMD would be
shipping Clawhammer parts at over 2 GHz which will run the simulator
even faster due to having more registers and native 64-bit operations.

FPGAs get faster too, but they aren't on as steep of a curve, and there
isn't as much compatability from one generation of FPGAs to the next.
There's compatability at the Verilog or VHDL level, but that doesn't
get you any guarantee of performance scaling - you have to reoptimize
the design and placement for each new family of FPGAs, or you won't gain
much.  By way of comparison, the latest P4 and Athlon processors can
still run 8086 code dating back to 1978.

Trying to use special-purpose hardware to compete with general purpose
CPUs is like trying to run in front of a steamroller.  You might be able
to do it for a short while, but you're going to get tired before the
steamroller does.  This is why LISP machines were ultimately
unsuccessful.

Of course, I'd be delighted if any of the people currently working
on FPGA PDP-10s would prove me wrong.  :-)

Eric


