I agree with Eric that beating a good emulator running on
a state-of-the-art processor with an FPGA based design would be somewhere
between difficult and impossible. The sea of general-purpose
lookup tables and flip-flops are not always what you want, and the
FPGA wiring is terribly slow.

My FPGA design isn't even trying to be fast; I'm building
it more to sit on the table beside the FPGA versions of the DEC 12-bit and
18-bit machines which are already done. As such, it's a very simple
design, with an 18-bit datapath explicitly double-pumped by
fairly vertical microcode. It current does JRST . at about 3.5 MIPS and
continuous blocks of something like ADD at about 2 MIPS, but I'm about to
change the microcode format a little to improve the access to
memory operands which are in an AC, to make the instruction dispatch
faster, and to make returning to instruction fetch and dispatch essentially
free. The 18-bit datapath isn't as terrible as you might
think, except for things like multiple and divide; the 72-bit shifts are
a little wordy, so say the least.

dgc

