It is certainly true that the question of how many users a given
TOPS-10/Tops-20/VMS system could support satisfactorily depended
on a great many things, including memory, I/O load and configuration,
choice of terminal and network connectivity devices, what the users
were doing, etc. etc.

However, back in my younger days I had a great deal to do with the
TOPS-10 scheduler, which my boss and I based on a model by Klienrock
(seems there was a second name, but I forget) and with very extensive
testing to ensure that it actually followed the model.  It went to
considerable effort to ensure "fairness" to all the users.

One of the big issues which was required to make it behave fairly was
to ensure that it really did give out a time slice and that the user
had a fair chance to use it.  The earlier scheduler moved everyone
around in priority constantly, based on what I/O or resource they had
just completed.  The result was very random behavior, with everyone
constantly going to the front of the priority queue.  Under the Klienrock/WMU
model, no one EVER was requed to the front of a priority queue.  Once you
reached the top, you stayed there until you either got you quantum/slice
or spend too long doing other things without using it, after which you
went to the bottom of the queue again.

The result of this was that the OS could give very responsive service
to a diverse mix of users, even when very short on memory by VMS standards.

VMS, at least in those early days (I haven't seen internals documents
lately), followed the model of constantly revising everyone's priority. 
The result we often saw, and which others also commented on, was that
when there was a mix of interactive users, heavy I/O user's, and CPU
intensive users, response was extremely erratic.  If you asked for a
quick response, which might require half a dozen I/Os, you would get
an I/O, wait for all the cpu bound jobs, an I/O, wait for the CPU bound
jobs, ...  This was frequently a pain -- unless you could force all of
the CPU jobs to lower priorities.

This isn't to say that TOPS-10 scheduling was perfect. Particularly
if you used the class scheduler, just choosing who to run could easily
consume 35% or more of the system.  And class scheduling overhead got
worse as memory got bigger, because there were more jobs to choose
between.

Anyway, this may explain why people don't think VMS could timeshare.

Personally, as upset as we were when DEC canceled Jupiter (I can still
remember the DEC VP in St. Louis [what was her name?] being escorted
around all week by at least two armed guards], I much prefer the
reliability and stability of VMS and Alpha.  But it took way to long
to get there.

And, my own judgement on HP/Compaq is that the merger will destroy
both companies.


