Sorry.  I may have used bad terminology.  A great part of the
expertise in my area was based on being able to think about the
customer _before_ generating bits.  I knew a lot of people who
could generate a lot of bits but, if they didn't provide computing
service that the customers could use, they were essentially random
bits.  Operating system development is not all programming.  There's
a lot more involved.  That's what I was talking about.  Listening
to the customer was crucial to this biz.  Now, this does not mean
that the customer was correct in what he was saying.  A lot of times
he wasn't because he was presenting a solution rather than describing
a problem.  A good part of being an expert OS developer is figuring
out the problem using what the customer said and then extrapolating
it to a general solution.  Solving one customer's problem was rarely
a solution for all customers.  

/BAH

