In article <3CAD85DD.24280101@aerosys.co.uk>,
 Chris Quayle <lightwork@aerosys.co.uk> writes:
|> Bill Todd wrote:
|> 
|> > 
|> > Sorry for the ambiguous antecedent:  the 'latter date' referred to was 1983.
|> > By 1987, the writing was on the wall for the rest of the world to see - as
|> > evidenced by the fact that, unlike most of the rest of the market, the DEC
|> > stock price never came close to recovering from the October '87 crash.
|> > 
|> 
|> There were other issues, one of which was the major shift in emphasis
|> from big iron to distributed systems that started in the mid to late
|> eighties. 

Can't lay the blame there unless your blaming DEC for not seeing and
reacting to the change.  I was involved in a very early "distributed
data proceessing" project in 1981.  Even got my picture in the USMA
newspaper.  It involved the off-loading of all admissions data entry
from the UNIVAC-1100 mainframe onto micros that emulated the user
interface the keypunch clerks were familiar with.  What does this have
to do with DEC??  The micros were LSI-11/02's.  

|>            Client server became fashionable with the availabily of
|> reliable networking 

It started before and didn't rely on networking as we know it today.
We did with a Gandalf switch.  As a matter of fact, the primary force
driving this project was to get slow users (data entry) away from tying
up a Gandalf port (a very limited resource) all day long.  By moving
the data entry and validation onto a micro a whole days data input could
be uploaded att he end of the day in less than 1 half hour.

|>                      and companies like Sun were selling desktop
|> workstations that could wee all over some of the Dec big iron of the
|> time at a fraction of the cost. 

This was one of the biggest problems we saw.  If we did nto already have
a ready supply of these LSI-11's (Teraks) the micro end would never have
been DEC.  

|>                                 The OS's were nothing like as good as
|> VMS, but few beancounters noticed this, or the increased cost of
|> support.

And while you keep harping on this, it still isn't true.  A Sun was
no harder to administer than a VAX running VMS.  And the availability
of people with UNIX experience over those with VMS experience was
already significant.

|> 
|> A good indicator is the s/hand dealer market. Many people made a fair
|> living from s/hand pdp and vax kit into the eighties and the market was
|> stable, with prices high.  After the recession of the early 90's, there
|> was a veritable glut of such kit. Many dealers went out of business and
|> much of the kit ended up as scrap metal because it was unsaleable at any
|> price. All in the space of 1-2 years...


I got news for you, those prices are still high.  Go to any of the dealers
on the INTERNET and price a DEUNA.  And these guys are still doing business.  
While hobbyists like me are willing to pick up anybodys old parts businesses
don't do that and VAX and even PDP's are still very much in use today.  Some
of the kit has been modernized, mostly moving from RA disks to SCSI, but the
systems are still running out there.

|> 
|> Though DEC failed to see the rising tide, there was little that could be
|> done quickly against such a change in paradigm and a recession that
|> destroyed thousands of previously healthy businesses.

It seems to me that in some cases it was not moving too slow but moving
too fast that hurt the most.  Writting off lines that still had plenty
of life left in them.  Maybe one of the reasons Wintel systems are getting
more and more use in the server world is because everyone doesn't need
a supercomputer and isn't willing to pay the price for CPU cycles that
fall into the bit-bucket.

bill

