Mark Crispin <mrc@CAC.Washington.EDU> writes:

> On Wed, 3 Apr 2002, Hans Vlems wrote:
> > one question: how many 10/20's did DEC/Digital sell and how many VMS
> > systems?
> > That should put things in perspective, wouldn't it.
> 
> A better measure is:
> 
> How many 10/20s were on the Internet, and how many VMS systems?
> 
> VMS did not replace the 10/20 on the Internet.  UNIX did.
> 
> -- Mark --

From the perspective of a very large ARPANET site, the handwriting was on
the wall.  Mainframes were on the way out as research workhorses by 1980.

PARC had captured most of the architectural action.  Even AMD bit-slice
architecture could deliver more cycles in a workstation to the user than
could a mainframe shared among many users, let alone provide good
bitmap/mouse support ... and microprocessors were obviously coming along.

The thing that really galled, was that those with a decent grasp of the
hardware/software advances knew which direction to take DEC.  But corporate
apparently wasn't interested in providing a 20 workstation, if indeed
any good workstation then.

It was very sad to see the calcification of a once leading-edge company.
The R&D community had great fondness for Tops-20/TENEX.  Two years were
spent thrashing around, wasting time and money looking for a suitable
workstation.  Several companies produced workstation products in the very
early 80's.  Stanford (SUN), CMU (Perq), Apple (Lisa), BBN (Jericho), Xerox
(Star), Apollo ... I forget some of the others.  Developing expertise on an
entirely new machine architectures wastes immense time and energy.  Much of
the ARPANET community was effectively forced to leave DEC behind ... and we
didn't like that.

SUN/Unix won because it was general purpose platform, open source (BSD-based
and easily licensed initially) and a multi-lingual system.  ARPA had to pay
to make UNIX a sufficient base.  DEC had become a legacy system for the ARPA
research community and it was doomed ... notwithstanding the superb work on
Alpha and ARM years later.

All these years later and we still don't have GTJFN, COMND and ...
Lots was lost.

--- ggf


