On 5 Apr 2002, Greg Finn wrote:
> The thing that really galled, was that those with a decent grasp of the
> hardware/software advances knew which direction to take DEC.  But corporate
> apparently wasn't interested in providing a 20 workstation, if indeed
> any good workstation then.

Yup.  The 2020 was ridiculously slow, but it proved that it was possible
to build a non-mainframe PDP-10.  Foonly proved that it could be done
cheaply.  There were the lost opportunities... notably Dolphin on the high
end and Minnow on the low end.

But DEC corporate policy was first to prevent product lines competiting
with each other.  Later it was changed so that VAX was permitted to
compete with other product lines, but not the other way around.

> It was very sad to see the calcification of a once leading-edge company.
> The R&D community had great fondness for Tops-20/TENEX.  Two years were
> spent thrashing around, wasting time and money looking for a suitable
> workstation.

I remember that time quite well...

> SUN/Unix won because it was general purpose platform, open source (BSD-based
> and easily licensed initially) and a multi-lingual system.  ARPA had to pay
> to make UNIX a sufficient base.

It also had become clear that there really wasn't much of a choice.  Few
people really liked UNIX; there were a few bigots but the majority went to
UNIX because there was no other choice.

DECWRL built a fairly pleasant UNIX, and there was an opportunity when SUN
jumped on the SVR4 bandwagon, but then Digital decided to go off on their
own with OSF/1.

> All these years later and we still don't have GTJFN, COMND and ...
> Lots was lost.

On the other hand, there is Lingling, which I believe to be the fastest
TOPS-20 system ever at 15x KL speed (about the equivalent of a 45 MHz
Intel) and put together for a cost of about $750.  I imagine that with a
more suitable hardware base (the microcode incurs a 33x performance hit,
which is actually pretty good -- 100x is considered more typical of an
emulator), we could have a real kick-ass system.

Anyway, having GTJFN, COMND, etc. back again is quite enjoyable.

-- Mark --

